Q. Difference between C and C++
A: Key Points
C is a simple, fast, procedural language mainly used for system programming, embedded systems, and operating systems.
C++ is an extension of C that adds Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) features and many advanced capabilities.
Most C programs can be compiled in C++, but not all C++ programs can run as C programs.
In short:
C focuses on functions and procedures, while C++ focuses on objects, classes, and code reusability.

